\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Generalized Least Squares Estimator (GLSE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
Violation of the Gauss-Markov Assumptions - Nonshperical Covaraince: 
\series default
Effects on the OLSE - inefficient, Estimation method - Generalized Least
 Squares (GLS) estimator, Special forms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, Estimation and Test of Heteroskedastic Models, Estimation and Tests of
 Autoregressive Errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Violation of the Gauss-Markov Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Nonspherical Covariance of the Error Term
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
The Gauss-Markov Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Gauss-Markov assumptions on the linear model 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta+u$
\end_inset

 :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(A1) 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(X)=K,\ rank(y,X)=k+1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(A2) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|X)=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(A3) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption 3 states that the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

 matrix of conditional second moments 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)(=cov(u|X))$
\end_inset

 is spherical, that is, proportional to the identity matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Violation of (A3)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Without (A3), each element of the 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times N$
\end_inset

 matrix is in general a nonlinear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 If the error is not (conditionally) homoskedastic, the values of the diagonal
 elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)$
\end_inset

 are not the same, and if there is correlation in the error term between
 observations (the case of serial correlation for time-series models), the
 values of the off-diagonal elements are not zero.
 For any given positive scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, define 
\begin_inset Formula $V(X)\equiv\nicefrac{E(uu^{\prime}|X)}{\sigma^{2}}$
\end_inset

 and asme 
\begin_inset Formula $V(X)$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular and known.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(A3^{*}):E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}V(X)_{N\times N}\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The reason we decompose 
\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)$
\end_inset

 into the component 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is common to all elements of the matrix 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)$
\end_inset

 and the remaining component 
\begin_inset Formula $V(X)$
\end_inset

 is that we do not need to know the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 for efficient estimation.
 The model that results when (A3) is replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ref{eq:1})$
\end_inset

, which merely assumes that the conditional second moment 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit

\begin_inset Formula $E(uu^{\prime}|X)$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, is called the 
\series bold
generalized regression model
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Consequences of Relaxing (A3)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The effects on the OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Linear Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y=\beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Unbiased Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{\beta}|X)=\beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}E(u|X)=\beta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Inefficeint Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\hat{\beta}|X)=cov[(\beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u|X]=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}cov(u|X)X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}=\sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega X(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}\neq\sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{\sigma}^{2}|X)=E\left[\frac{\hat{u}^{\prime}\hat{u}}{N-K}|X\right]=\frac{E\left(u^{\prime}Mu|X\right)}{N-K}=\frac{tr[ME(uu^{\prime}|X)]}{N-K}=\frac{\sigma^{2}tr(M\Omega)}{N-K}\neq\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is still a linear and unbiased estimator, but it is no longer efficient
 since the Gauss-Markov Theorem no longer holds for the OLSE.
 The BLUE is now some other estimator.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The OLSE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is biased because 
\begin_inset Formula $tr(M\Omega)\neq(N-K)$
\end_inset

 in general.
 Also, the usual form 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\hat{\beta}|X)$
\end_inset

 is incorrect, and hence, any statistical inference based on its estimate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}$
\end_inset

 will be incorrect also.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-ratio is not distributed as the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 Thus, the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-test is no longer valid.
 The same comments apply to the 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

-test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Efficient Estimation with Known V
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the value of the matrix function 
\begin_inset Formula $V(X)$
\end_inset

 is known, does there exist a BLUE for the generalized regression model?
 The answer is yes, and the estimator is called the 
\series bold
generalized least squares (GLS) estimator
\series default
, which we now derive.
 The basic idea of the derivation is to transform the generalized regression
 model, which consists of (A1), (A2), and (A3*), into a model that satisfies
 all the assumptions, including (A3), of the classical regression model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For economy of notation, we use 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 for the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $V(X)$
\end_inset

.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is, by construction, symmetric and positive definite.
 So, (A3*) can be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(A3^{*}):E(uu^{\prime}|X)=\sigma^{2}\Omega,\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is a symmetric positive definite matrix of constants, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the scaling paramter such that 
\begin_inset Formula $tr(\Omega)=N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a result, there exists a nonsingular 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times N$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P^{\prime}P=\Omega^{-1}\label{eq:3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This decomposition is not unique, with more than once choice for 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

, but, as is clear from the discussion below, the choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 doesn't matter.
 Important to note, the OLS minimizes the unweighted SSR, while the GLS
 minimizes a weighted SSR, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{OLS:}\ {\displaystyle \min_{\beta}(y-X\beta)^{\prime}(y-X\beta)}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{GLS:}\ {\displaystyle \min_{\beta}(y-X\beta)^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}(y-X\beta)}\label{eq:4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, consider creating a new regression model by transforming 
\begin_inset Formula $(y,X,u)$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y^{*}\equiv Py,\ X^{*}\equiv PX,\ u^{*}\equiv Pu\label{eq:5}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since we estimating the standard linear regression model, this implies that
 
\begin_inset Formula $(y^{*},X^{*},u^{*})$
\end_inset

 too satisfies linearity: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y^{*}=X^{*}\beta+u^{*}\label{eq:6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The transformed model satisfies the other assumptions of the standard linear
 regression model.
 Strict exogeneity is satisfied because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(u^{*}|X^{*}) & = & E(u^{\star}|X)\\
 &  & \mbox{(since \ensuremath{P}is nonsingular, \ensuremath{X}and \ensuremath{X^{*}}contain the same information)}\\
 & = & PE(u|X)\\
 & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u|X)=0\ \mbox{by (A2))}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is positive definite, the no multicollinearity assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $(rank(X)=K,\ rank(y,X)=K+1)$
\end_inset

 is also satisfied.
 Assmumption 3 is satisfied for the transformed model because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(u^{*}u^{*\prime}|X^{*}) & = & E(u^{*}u^{*\prime}|X)\ \ \mbox{(since \ensuremath{X}and \ensuremath{X^{*}}contain the same information)}\\
 & = & E(Puu^{\prime}P^{\prime}|X)\\
 & = & PE(uu^{\prime}|X)P^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}P\Omega P^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}P(P^{\prime}P)^{-1}P^{\prime}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \Omega=(P^{\prime}P)^{-1}\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}PP^{-1}\left(P^{\prime}\right)^{-1}P^{\prime}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}I
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So indeed the covariance of the transformed error vector 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{*}$
\end_inset

 is spherical.
 Finally, 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{*}|X^{*}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}I)$
\end_inset

 because the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{*}|X^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{*}|X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{*}$
\end_inset

is a linear transformation of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 This completes the verification of Assumptions (A1)-(A4).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Gauss-Markov Theorem for the transformed model implies that the BLUE
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 for the generalized regression model is the OLS estimator applied to (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:6"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}_{GLS} & = & (X^{*\prime}X^{*})^{-1}X^{*\prime}y^{*}\nonumber \\
 & = & (X^{\prime}P^{\prime}PX)^{-1}X^{\prime}P^{\prime}Py\nonumber \\
 & = & (X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}y\label{eq:7}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

This is the GLS estimator.
 Its conditional variance is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
cov(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}|X) & = & cov((X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}y|X)\nonumber \\
 & = & (X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}cov(y|X)\Omega^{-1}X(X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}\nonumber \\
 & = & (X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}(\sigma^{2}\Omega)\Omega^{-1}X(X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}\nonumber \\
 &  & \mbox{(since}\ cov(y|X)=cov(u|X)\mbox{)}\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma^{2}(X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}\label{eq:8}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}|X)$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
est.\ cov(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}|X)=\hat{\sigma}^{2}(X^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X)^{-1}\label{eq:9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{(y-X\hat{\beta}_{GLS})^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}(y-X\hat{\beta}_{GLS})}{N-K}\label{eq:10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is an unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}\cdot\Omega(=cov(u|X))$
\end_inset

 in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:7"

\end_inset

) does not change the numerical value, the GLS estimator can also be written
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GLS}=[X^{\prime}cov(u|X)^{-1}X]^{-1}X^{\prime}cov(u|X)^{-1}y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As noted above, the OLS estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y$
\end_inset

 is also unbiased without A3, but nevertheless the GLS estimator should
 be preferred (provided 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is known) because the latter is more efficient in that the variance is
 smaller in the matrix sense.
 The gain in efficiency is achieved by exploiting the heteroskedasticity
 and correlation between observations in the error term, which, operationally,
 is to insert the inverse of (a matrix proportional to) 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(u|X)$
\end_inset

 in the OLS formula, as in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:7"

\end_inset

) .
 The discussion so far can be summarized as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(finite-sample properties of GLS):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*
(a) (unbiasedness) Under Assumptions (A1) and (A2), 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}|X)=\beta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*
(b) (expression for the variance) Under Assumptions (A1) and (A2) and the
 assumption that the conditional second moment is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $V(X)$
\end_inset

, i.e.
 (A3*),
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}|X)=\sigma^{2}\cdot(X^{\prime}V(X)^{-1}X)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*
(c) (efficiency of GLS) Under the same set of assumptions as in (b), the
 GLS estimator is efficient in that the conditional covariance of any unbiased
 estimator that is linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is greater than or equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\hat{\beta}_{GLS}|X)$
\end_inset

 in the matrix sense.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Special Forms of Covariance for Known 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have presented the classical linear regression model, treating the regressors
 as random.
 For these examples, assume 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixed,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or deterministic, regressor.
 Then, there is no need to distinguish between the conditional distribution
 of the error term, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(u_{t}|X_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,X_{t}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, and the unconditional distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(u_{t})$
\end_inset

, so that Assumptions (A2) and (A3*) can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
(A2):E(u_{t}) & = & 0\label{eq:11}\\
(A3^{*}):E(uu^{\prime}) & = & \sigma^{2}\Omega\label{eq:12}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The assumption of fixed regressors remains popular because the regression
 model with fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 can be interpreted as a set of statements conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, allowing us to dispense with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $|X$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 from the statements.
 We will treat the regressors as random another time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
[1] Heteroskedasticity Only
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{tt}\neq\omega_{ss}$
\end_inset

 for at least one pair of 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ts}=0\ \forall\ t\neq s$
\end_inset

.
 In this case, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal matrix and its inverse is a diagonal matrix with 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{tt}^{-1}$
\end_inset

 as diagonal elements.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Omega=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\omega_{11} &  & \mathbf{0}\\
 & \ddots\\
\mathbf{0} &  & \omega_{NN}
\end{array}\right]_{(N\times N)},\ \Omega^{-1}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\omega_{11}^{-1} &  & \mathbf{0}\\
 & \ddots\\
\mathbf{0} &  & \omega_{NN}^{-1}
\end{array}\right]_{(N\times N)}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}=P^{\prime}P$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal matrix with 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{tt}^{-\frac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

 as diagonal elemnts, and hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{*}=\frac{y_{t}}{\sqrt{\omega_{tt}}}\ \mbox{and}\ X_{t}^{*}=\frac{X_{t}}{\sqrt{\omega_{tt}}}$
\end_inset

.
 The GLS estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\series bold
weighted least squares
\series default
 estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose there are 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{t}$
\end_inset

 number of firms in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and the linear model of interest for each firm in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{tj}=X_{tj}\beta+u_{tj}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J_{t}\ \mbox{and}\ t=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose this equation satisfies all Gauss-Markov assumptions, so that OLSE
 is the BLUE if data at the firm level are available.
 However, if only industry aggregate data are available and the number of
 firms in the industry changed over time, we will have a heteroskedasticity
 problem in the regression equation 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t}$
\end_inset

, where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}={\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J_{t}}y_{tj},}\ X_{t}=\sum_{j=1}^{J_{t}}X_{tj},\ u_{t}=\sum_{j=1}^{J_{t}}u_{tj}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
var(u_{t}) & = & var\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J_{t}}}u_{tj}\right)\right]\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J_{t}}}var(u_{tj})\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \ensuremath{cov(u_{ti},u_{tj}})=0\ \forall\ i\neq j\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma^{2}J_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
[2] Non-sperical Covariance with Homoskedasticity - Moving average data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t}$
\end_inset

 satisifies all the Gauss-Markov assumptions for the true quarterly data.
 Suppose that the published data is a 4-quarter moving average of the true
 data:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{m}=\frac{1}{4}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=0}^{3}y_{t-s}},\ X_{t}^{m}=\frac{1}{4}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=0}^{3}X_{t-s}},\ u_{t}^{m}=\frac{1}{4}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=0}^{3}u_{t-s}}
\]

\end_inset

Then, the error term in the regression equation 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{m}=X_{t}^{m}\beta+u_{t}^{m}$
\end_inset

 has a covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}\Omega$
\end_inset

, which is not diagonal:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
var(u_{t}^{m}) & = & var\left[\left(\frac{1}{4}\sum_{s=0}^{3}u_{t-s}\right)\right]\\
 & = & \frac{1}{16}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=0}^{3}var\left(u_{t-s}\right)\ \ \mbox{(since}\ cov(u_{t},u_{t-s})=0\ \mbox{for}\ s=1,2,3\mbox{)}}\\
 & = & \frac{4}{16}\sigma^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(u_{t}^{m},u_{t-1}^{m}) & = & E[(u_{t}^{m}-E(u_{t}^{m}))(u_{t-1}^{m}-E(u_{t-1}^{m}))]\\
 & = & E[(u_{t}^{m})(u_{t-1}^{m})]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u_{t}^{m})=E(u_{t-1}^{m})=0\mbox{)}\\
 & = & E\left[\frac{1}{4}(u_{t}+u_{t-1}+u_{t-2}+u_{t-3})\frac{1}{4}(u_{t-1}+u_{t-2}+u_{t-3}+u_{t-4})\right]\\
 & = & \frac{1}{16}\left[E(u_{t-1}^{2})+E(u_{t-2}^{2})+E(u_{t-3}^{2})\right]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u_{t}u_{s})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \frac{3}{16}\sigma^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the variance-covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{m}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u^{m})=\frac{\sigma^{2}}{16}\left[\begin{array}{cccccc}
4 & 3 & 2 & 1 & 0 & \ldots\\
3 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\
2 & 3 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\
1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 3 & 2\\
0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 2\\
\vdots & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{tt}=\frac{4}{16},\ \omega_{t,t+1}=\frac{3}{16},\ \omega_{t,t+2}=\frac{2}{16},\ \omega_{t,t+3}=\frac{1}{16},\ \omega_{t,t+s}=0\ \forall\ s>3$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
[3] Autocorrelation - Autoregressive (AR) error term
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
(a) First order Markov process AR(1)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=\rho u_{t-1}+\epsilon_{t},\ |\rho|<1,\ \epsilon_{t}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=\epsilon_{t}+\rho\epsilon_{t-1}+\rho^{2}\epsilon_{t-2}+\cdots+\rho^{n}\epsilon_{t-n}+\cdots$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
AR(1) is weakly stationary if 
\begin_inset Formula $|\rho|<1$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{t}$
\end_inset

 is known as 
\series bold
white noise
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sigma_{u}^{2}\equiv var(u_{t}) & = & var(\epsilon_{t}+\rho\epsilon_{t-1}+\rho^{2}\epsilon_{t-2}+\cdots+\rho^{n}\epsilon_{t-n}+\cdots)\\
 & = & var(\epsilon_{t})+\rho^{2}var(\epsilon_{t-1})+\cdots+\rho^{2n}var(\epsilon_{t-n})+\cdots\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \epsilon_{t}\ \mbox{are}\ i.i.d.,\ \mbox{then}\ cov(\epsilon_{t},\epsilon_{s})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}(1+\rho^{2}+\rho^{4}+\cdots+\rho^{2n}+\cdots)\ \ \mbox{(since}\ var(\epsilon_{t})=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\ \forall\ t\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \frac{\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}{1-\rho^{2}}\ \ \mbox{(since}\ |\rho|<1\mbox{)}\\
cov(u_{t},u_{t-1}) & = & E[(u_{t}-E(u_{t}))(u_{t-1}-E(u_{t-1}))]\\
 & = & E[(u_{t})(u_{t-1})]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u_{t})=E(u_{t-1})=0\mbox{)}\\
 & = & E[(\epsilon_{t}+\rho\epsilon_{t-1}+\rho\epsilon_{t-2}+\cdots+\rho^{n}\epsilon_{t-n}+\cdots)(\epsilon_{t-1}+\rho\epsilon_{t-2}+\cdots+\rho^{n}\epsilon_{t-n}+\cdots)]\\
 & = & \rho E(\epsilon_{t-1}^{2})+\rho^{3}E(\epsilon_{t-2}^{2})+\cdots\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \epsilon_{t}\ \mbox{are}\ i.i.d.,\ \mbox{then}\ E(\epsilon_{t}\epsilon_{s})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\rho(1+\rho^{2}+\rho^{4}+\cdots)\ \ \mbox{\mbox{(since}\ var(\epsilon_{t})=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\ \forall\ t\mbox{)}}\\
 & = & \rho\frac{\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}{1-\rho^{2}}\\
cov(u_{t},u_{t-n}) & = & \rho^{n}\left(\frac{\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}{1-\rho^{2}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the variance-covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u)=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\Omega=\left(\frac{\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}{1-\rho^{2}}\right)\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
1 & \rho & \rho^{2} & \cdots & \rho^{N-1}\\
\rho & 1 & \rho & \cdots & \rho^{N-2}\\
\rho^{2} & \rho & 1 & \cdots & \rho^{N-3}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots\\
\rho^{N-1} & \rho^{N-2} & \rho^{N-3} & \cdots & 1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

The transformation matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}P=\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P=\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}} & 0 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
-\rho & 1 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & -\rho & 1 & \cdots & 0\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
0 & 0 & \cdots & -\rho & 1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

which gives the data transformation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}^{*}=\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}}\cdot y_{1},\ y_{t}^{*}=y_{t}-\rho y_{t-1}\ \mbox{for}\ t=2,\ldots,N
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{1}^{*}=\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}}\cdot X_{1},\ X_{t}^{*}=X_{t}-\rho X_{t-1}\ \mbox{for}\ t=2,\ldots,N
\]

\end_inset

How did we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

? 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{t} & = & X_{t}\beta+u_{t}\\
y_{t} & = & X_{t}\beta+(\rho u_{t-1}+\epsilon_{t})\\
y_{t} & = & \rho y_{t-1}+X_{t}\beta-X_{t-1}(\rho\beta)+\epsilon_{t}\\
y_{t}-\rho y_{t-1} & = & (X_{t}-\rho X_{t-1})\beta+u_{t}-\rho u_{t-1}\\
y_{t}^{*} & = & X_{t}^{*}\beta+u_{t}^{*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We still need to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{u}_{t} & = & \rho\hat{u}_{t-1}+\epsilon_{t}\\
\hat{\rho} & = & \frac{\sum\hat{u}_{t}\hat{u}_{t-1}}{\sum\hat{u}_{t-1}^{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We get 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}$
\end_inset

 by running OLS on the original SLM, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=X_{t}\beta+u_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
(b) Second order autoregressive process AR(2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}=\rho_{1}u_{t-1}+\rho_{2}u_{t-2}+\epsilon_{t},\ \epsilon_{t}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{t}\}$
\end_inset

 is a stationary stochastic process, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t})=E(u_{s})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $var(u_{t})=var(u_{s})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(u_{t},u_{t-s})$
\end_inset

 depends only on 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 Using these properties, it is straightforward to verify 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{t})=0$
\end_inset

, and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
[4] Moving Average (MA) Process of the error term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First order MA process MA(1):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{t}=\epsilon_{t}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-1},\ |\alpha|<1,\ \epsilon_{t}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})
\]

\end_inset

These properties imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(u_{t}) & = & E(\epsilon_{t}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-1})\\
 & = & E(\epsilon_{t}|X)+\alpha E(\epsilon_{t-1})\\
 & = & 0\\
var(u_{t}) & = & var(\epsilon_{t}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-1})\\
 & = & var(\epsilon_{t})+\alpha^{2}var(\epsilon_{t-1})\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \epsilon_{t}\ \mbox{are}\ i.i.d.,\ \mbox{then}\ cov(\epsilon_{t},\epsilon_{s})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\alpha^{2}\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\\
 & = & (1+\alpha^{2})\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\\
cov(u_{t},u_{t-1}) & = & E[(u_{t}-E(u_{t}))(u_{t-1}-E(u_{t-1}))]\\
 & = & E[(u_{t})(u_{t-1})]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u_{t})=E(u_{t-1})=0)\\
 & = & E[(\epsilon_{t}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-1})(\epsilon_{t-1}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-2})]\\
 & = & \alpha E(\epsilon_{t-1})\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \epsilon_{t}\ \mbox{are}\ i.i.d.,\ \mbox{then}\ E(\epsilon_{t}\epsilon_{s})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\mbox{)}\\
 & = & \alpha\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\\
cov(u_{t},u_{t-s}) & = & E[(u_{t}-E(u_{t}))(u_{t-s}-E(u_{t-s}))]\\
 & = & E[(u_{t})(u_{t-s})]\ \ \mbox{(since}\ E(u_{t})=E(u_{t-s})=0)\\
 & = & E[(\epsilon_{t}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-1})(\epsilon_{t-s}+\alpha\epsilon_{t-s-1})]\\
 & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(since}\ \epsilon_{t}\ \mbox{are}\ i.i.d.,\ \mbox{then}\ cov(\epsilon_{t},\epsilon_{s})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\mbox{)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the conditional covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u)=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\Omega=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
(1+\alpha^{2}) & \alpha & 0 & \cdots\\
\alpha & (1+\alpha^{2}) & \alpha\\
0 & \alpha & \ddots\\
\vdots
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The square root matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}P=\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

 involves trigonometric functions.
 One can find 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 by the Choleski decomposition.
 Alternatrivley, let 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 be the matrix of eigenvectors (characteristic vectors) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 be the diagonal matrix with the eigenvalues (characteristic roots) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

 at the diagonal.
 That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}C=C\Lambda$
\end_inset

.
 Then, let 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\Lambda^{\frac{1}{2}}C^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}P=C\Lambda C^{\prime}=\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
[6] Seemingly Unrelated Regression (SUR) Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we have two equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{j}=X_{j}\beta_{j}+u_{j},\ j=1,2
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of dependent variables, and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times K_{j}$
\end_inset

 matrix of exogenous variables.
 The error terms have the moments
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{j})=0,\ E(u_{j}u_{k})=\sigma_{jk}I
\]

\end_inset

Each error term statisfies the Gauss-Markov assumptions, and they are 
\series bold
contemporaneously
\series default
 correlated, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u_{tj},u_{tk})=\sigma_{jk},\ cov(u_{tj},u_{sk})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Separate estimation of each equation does not take this correlation into
 account, and will be less efficient than an estimator that utiltizes it.
 To estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 jointly, stack 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 i the way similar to the setup of the data matrix when we test stability
 of the coefficients.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
y_{2}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
X_{1} & 0\\
0 & X_{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{1}\\
\beta_{2}
\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{c}
u_{1}\\
u_{2}
\end{array}\right]\longrightarrow y=X\beta+u
\]

\end_inset

If the stacked-up error vector 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 has a scalar covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset

, the OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 will be BLUE.
 However, because of the contemporaneous correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(u|X)$
\end_inset

 is not of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the conditional covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

?
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u)=E(uu^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
E(u_{1}u_{1}^{\prime}) & E(u_{1}u_{2}^{\prime})\\
E(u_{2}u_{1}^{\prime}) & E(u_{2}u_{2}^{\prime})
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma_{11}I & \sigma_{12}I\\
\sigma_{21}I & \sigma_{22}I
\end{array}\right]=\Sigma\otimes I,\ \Sigma=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma_{11} & \sigma_{12}\\
\sigma_{21} & \sigma_{22}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remarks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

 is the contemporaneous covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{t1},u_{t2})^{\prime}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) The GLS estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GLS}=\left[X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)X{}^{-1}\right]X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y
\]

\end_inset

We obtain an explicit estimate by running a two step estimation process:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run OLS on each equation to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{1}\ \mbox{and}\ \hat{u}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{jk}=\frac{\hat{u}_{j}^{\prime}\hat{u}_{k}}{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run GLS with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Sigma}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) GLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identical to the separate OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 from each equation if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{12}=\sigma_{21}=0$
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

 is diagonal), or if 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}\equiv X_{2}$
\end_inset

 (i.e., data of the regressors are identical).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
To show the first case 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{12}=\sigma_{21}=0)$
\end_inset

, recall that the inverse of a diagonal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

 is simply taking the inverse of the diagonal elemetns.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{GLS} & = & \left[X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)X{}^{-1}\right]X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y\\
 & = & \left(\left[\begin{array}{cc}
X_{1}^{\prime} & 0\\
0 & X_{2}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma_{11}^{-1}I & 0\\
0 & \sigma_{22}^{-1}I
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
X_{1} & 0\\
0 & X_{2}
\end{array}\right]\right)^{-1}\left[\begin{array}{cc}
X_{1}^{\prime} & 0\\
0 & X_{2}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma_{11}^{-1}I & 0\\
0 & \sigma_{22}^{-1}I
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
y_{2}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
\sigma_{11}\left(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1}\right)^{-1} & 0\\
0 & \sigma_{22}\left(X_{2}^{\prime}X_{2}\right)^{-1}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\sigma_{11}^{-1}X_{1}^{\prime}y_{1}\\
\sigma_{22}^{-1}X_{2}^{\prime}y_{2}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
\left(X_{1}^{\prime}X_{1}\right)^{-1}X_{1}^{\prime}y_{1}\\
\left(X_{2}^{\prime}X_{2}\right)^{-1}X_{2}^{\prime}y_{2}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{\beta}_{1\ OLS}\\
\hat{\beta}_{2\ OLS}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
To show the second case, let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z=X_{1}=X_{2}$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $X=I\otimes Z$
\end_inset

.
 Then, using the relationships 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\otimes B)^{\prime}=(A^{\prime}\otimes B^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(A\otimes B)(C\otimes D)=(AC\otimes BD)$
\end_inset

, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta}_{GLS} & = & \left[X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)X{}^{-1}\right]X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y\\
 & = & \left[\left(I\otimes Z^{\prime}\right)(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)(I\otimes Z)\right]^{-1}\left(I\otimes Z^{\prime}\right)(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y\\
 & = & \left[\Sigma^{-1}\otimes Z^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes Z^{\prime})y\\
 & = & [I\otimes(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}]y\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{cc}
(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime} & 0\\
0 & (Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
y_{2}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y_{1}\\
(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}y_{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(d) The extension to the SUR model with more than two equations is straightforwa
rd.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
[6] Time Series
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is the data set on different individuals or firms or countries over
 several time periods.
 This is essentially the same as the SUR model in that we have several equations.
 The name 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

time series - cross section data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 applies when we have observations on a few corss sectional units over a
 relatively long time periods.
 When we have observations on a large number of crosss sectional units over
 relatively short time periods, it is called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

panel data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

longitudinal data set.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The main feature of interest about the cross sectional units is the heterogenei
ty, while the times series dimension is interested in the stochastic properties
 over time.
 The emphasisi of the analysis typically depends on the nature of the data
 set.
 For example, the analysiss of a panel data is mainly interested in the
 heterogeneity, and there are a variety of ways to take that into account.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Time Series - Cross Section Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a time series - cross section data which has observations on 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 cross sectional units over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods, and in general, 
\begin_inset Formula $n\leq T$
\end_inset

.
 Consider a simple model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{ti}=X_{ti}\beta+u_{ti},\ i=1,\ldots,n,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

Note that, unlike the SUR model, this specification assumes a stable coefficeint
 over time as well as across cross sectional units.
 We can arrange the data in matrix forms in either over time or across the
 cross sectional units.
 We will use the popular convention of having the data matrices vary across
 corss sectional units, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of observations on agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 periods.
 Using this method, it is easier to specify the stochastic property over
 time.
 We can write the equation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{th}$
\end_inset

agent as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,n
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times K$
\end_inset

 observation matrix of the regressors for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Stacking these equations up as in the case of the SUR model, we can write
 for all agents over the entire periods as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1}\\
\vdots\\
y_{n}
\end{array}\right] & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
X_{1}\\
\vdots\\
X_{n}
\end{array}\right]\beta+\left[\begin{array}{c}
u_{1}\\
\vdots\\
u_{n}
\end{array}\right]\longrightarrow y=X\beta+u,\ \ V=cov(u)=E(uu^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\sigma_{11}\Omega_{11} & \cdots & \sigma_{1n}\Omega_{1n}\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\sigma_{n1}\Omega_{n1} & \cdots & \sigma_{nn}\Omega_{nn}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where each 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

-dimensional vectors and 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(u_{i},u_{j})=\sigma_{ij}\Omega_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 covariance matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that in the SUR model we imposed restrictions 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ij}=I\ \forall\ i,j$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption implies that there is neither own serial correlation in
 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

, nor corss equational serial correlation.
 By relaxing these restictions, or by imposing furhter restictions, we can
 construct a variety of models as discussed below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(a) Gauss-Markov model: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma^{2})\ \forall\ t,\ \mbox{and}\ i$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{ii}=\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ii}=I$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{ij}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ij}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\neq j$
\end_inset

.
 Therfore, 
\begin_inset Formula $V=\sigma^{2}I$
\end_inset

 and OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is BLUE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(b) Cross sectional heteroskedasticity only: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{ii})\ \forall\ t\ \mbox{and}\ i$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ii}=I$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{ij}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ij}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\neq j$
\end_inset

.
 The weighted least squares estimator in the standard case of heteroskedasticity
 will be the BLUE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(c) Cross sectional heteroskedasticity and contemporaneous correlation only
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
cov(u_{ti},u_{sj})=\begin{cases}
\sigma_{ij} & \mbox{if}\ t=s\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ t\neq s
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

This implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ij}=I$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\ \mbox{and}\ j$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $V=\Sigma\otimes I$
\end_inset

.
 The GLSE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{GLS}=\left[X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)X{}^{-1}\right]X^{\prime}(\Sigma^{-1}\otimes I)y$
\end_inset

 is the BLUE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(d) Individual Autocorrelation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}=\rho_{i}u_{t-1,i}+\epsilon_{ti}\ \forall\ i$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}=\epsilon_{ti}+\rho\epsilon_{t-1,i}+\rho^{2}\epsilon_{t-2,i}+\cdots+\rho^{n}\epsilon_{t-n,i}+\cdots$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two cases are presented below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
(i) 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ti}\sim i.i.d.(0,\tau_{ii})\ \forall\ t\ \mbox{and}\ i\ (\mbox{that is},\ cov(\epsilon_{ti},\epsilon_{sj})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s\ \mbox{and}\ i\neq j)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $var(u_{ti})=\sigma_{ii}=\frac{\tau_{ii}}{(1-\rho_{i}^{2})}\ \forall\ i,\ \mbox{and}\ \sigma_{ij}=0\ \mbox{for}\ i\neq j$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ii}$
\end_inset

 is the usual 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 matrix of AR(1) with 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the place of 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{ij}=0\ \forall\ i\neq j$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is a block diagonal matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
(ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $cov(\epsilon_{ti},\epsilon_{sj})=\tau_{ij}\ \mbox{for}\ t=s,\ \mbox{and}\ cov(\epsilon_{ti},\epsilon_{sj})=0\ \forall\ t\neq s$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{ti}$
\end_inset

 
\series medium
and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{si}$
\end_inset

 are contemporaneously correlated)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The diagonal blocs 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{ii}\Omega_{ii}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 are the same as in Case 
\series bold
(i)
\series default
.
 The off-diagonal blocks in 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 have the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{ij}\Omega_{ij}=\frac{\tau_{ij}}{1-\rho_{i}\rho_{j}}\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
1 & \rho_{j} & \rho_{j}^{2} & \cdots & \rho_{j}^{T-1}\\
\rho_{i} & 1 & \rho_{j} & \cdots & \rho_{j}^{T-2}\\
\rho_{i}^{2} & \rho_{i} & 1 & \cdots & \rho_{j}^{T-3}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots &  & \vdots\\
\rho_{i}^{T-1} & \rho_{i}^{T-2} & \rho_{i}^{T-3} & \cdots & 1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

This can be verified by considering either
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(u_{ti},u_{sj}) & = & cov(\rho_{i}u_{t-1,i}+\varepsilon_{ti},\rho_{j}u_{s-1,j}+\varepsilon_{sj}\\
 & = & \rho_{i}\rho_{j}cov(u_{t-1,i},u_{s-1,j})+\rho_{i}cov(u_{t-1,i},\varepsilon_{sj})+\rho_{j}cov(u_{s-1,j},\varepsilon_{ti})+cov(\varepsilon_{ti},\varepsilon_{sj})\\
 & \mbox{or}\\
cov(u_{ti},u_{sj}) & = & cov\left({\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}p_{i}^{k}\varepsilon_{t-k,i},{\displaystyle \sum_{l=0}^{\infty}}p_{i}^{l}\varepsilon_{s-l,j}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Here is an example that calculates a few elements of the matrix:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(u_{ti},u_{sj}) & = & cov\left[\left(\varepsilon_{ti}+\rho_{i}\epsilon_{t-1,i}+\rho_{i}^{2}\epsilon_{t-2,i}+\cdots\right),\left(\varepsilon_{sj}+\rho_{j}\epsilon_{s-1,j}+\rho_{j}^{2}\varepsilon_{s-2,j}+\cdots\right)\right]\\
 & = & E\left[\left(\varepsilon_{ti}+\rho_{i}\varepsilon_{t-1,i}+\rho_{i}^{2}\varepsilon_{t-2,i}+\cdots\right)\left(\varepsilon_{sj}+\rho_{j}\varepsilon_{s-1,j}+\rho_{j}^{2}\varepsilon_{s-2,j}+\cdots\right)\right]\ \ \mbox{since}\ E(\varepsilon_{ti})=0\ \forall\ t,i\\
 & = & E\left[\left(\varepsilon_{ti}\varepsilon_{sj}+\varepsilon_{ti}\rho_{j}\varepsilon_{s-1,j}+\cdots\right)+\left(\rho_{i}\varepsilon_{t-1,i}\varepsilon_{sj}+\rho_{i}\rho_{j}\varepsilon_{t-1,i}\varepsilon_{s-1,j}+\cdots\right)+\cdots\right]\\
t=s & : & \tau_{ij}(1+\rho_{i}\rho_{j}+\rho_{i}^{2}\rho_{j}^{2}+\cdots)=\frac{\tau_{ij}}{1-\rho_{i}\rho_{j}}\\
t=s-1 & : & \tau_{ij}\rho_{j}(1+\rho_{i}\rho_{j}+\cdots)=\frac{\tau_{ij}}{1-\rho_{i}\rho_{j}}\rho_{j}\\
t-1=s & : & \tau_{ij}\rho_{i}(1+\rho_{i}\rho_{j}+\cdots)=\frac{\tau_{ij}}{1-\rho_{i}\rho_{j}}\rho_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Longitudinal Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As noted earlier, the main interest in the analysis of a panel data is the
 heterogeneity across the corss sectional agents.
 There are two basic models that consider the cross sectional heterogeneity:
 
\series bold
Fixed individual effect model
\series default
 and 
\series bold
Random individual effect model
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(a) Fixed Effects Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The simplest form of this model assumes that the heterogeneity arises only
 in the intercept term of the regression equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{ti}=\alpha_{i}+X_{ti}\beta+u_{ti},\ i=1,\ldots,n,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ti}$
\end_inset

 now does not include a constant term.
 This is precisely the form of one unstable coefficient in the stability
 test of a subset of the coefficients.
 There is nothing new, except that we may now allow various properties of
 the error term in addition to the 
\begin_inset Formula $i.i.d.$
\end_inset

 case we considered for the atability tests.
 This can also be interpreted as a SUR model with restrictions across equations,
 i.e., the coefficients of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 are the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
(b) Random Effects Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This model assumes that the heterogeneity effect 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not constant, but a random variable with mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and a finite variance.
 This model views that the cross sectional sample is drawn from a large
 population whose individual effects have a certain distribution.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}=\alpha+\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{ti}=\alpha_{i}+X_{ti}\beta+u_{ti}=\alpha+X_{ti}\beta+(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti})=\alpha+X_{ti}\beta+\xi_{ti}
\]

\end_inset

and the random term 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{ti}$
\end_inset

 has two components: individual effect 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 which stays the same over time, and the pure random noise 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can construct a variety of models by considering different properties
 of the error terms 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}$
\end_inset

.
 The simplest model assumes that 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ti}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{ti}$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\epsilon_{i} & \sim & i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2})\ \forall\ i\\
u_{ti} & \sim & i.i.d.(0,\sigma_{u}^{2})\ \forall\ t\ \mbox{and}\ i\\
cov(\epsilon_{i},u_{t}) & = & 0\ \forall\ i,j,t
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Under these assumptions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
cov(\xi_{ti},\xi_{sj}) & = & cov[(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti}),(\epsilon_{j}+u_{si})]\\
 & = & E[(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti})(\epsilon_{j}+u_{si})]-E[(\epsilon_{i}+u_{ti})]E[(\epsilon_{j}+u_{si})]\\
 & = & E[\epsilon_{i}\epsilon_{j}+\epsilon_{i}u_{si}+\epsilon_{j}u_{ti}+u_{ti}u_{sj}]\\
 & = & \begin{cases}
\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2} & \mbox{if}\ t=s,\ i=j\\
\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2} & \mbox{if}\ t\neq s,\ i=j\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

All 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vectos of 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{i}$
\end_inset

 have a common covariance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Omega=cov(\xi_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I+\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}ee^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vecots of 1's.
 Therefore, the 
\begin_inset Formula $nT\times1$
\end_inset

 stacked-up vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi=(\xi_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,\xi_{n}^{\prime})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 has the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $V=I\otimes\Omega$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To transform the data for the GLS estimation of the stacked-up data matrices
 we need to find a transformation matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $P^{\prime}P=V^{-1}=I\otimes\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 be the square root matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $Q^{\prime}Q=\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $P=I\otimes Q$
\end_inset

.
 The square root matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q=\sigma_{u}^{-1}\left(I-\frac{\delta}{T}ee^{\prime}\right),\ \delta=1-\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sqrt{T\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

For the stacked-up 
\begin_inset Formula $nT\times1$
\end_inset

 vector 
\begin_inset Formula $y=(y_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,y_{n}^{\prime})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, each subvector of the transformed dependent variable in 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}=Py=(y_{1}^{*\prime},\ldots,y_{n}^{*\prime})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}^{*}=Qy_{i}=\sigma_{u}^{-1}\left(I-\frac{\delta}{T}ee^{\prime}\right)y_{i}=\sigma_{u}^{-1}(y_{i}-\delta e\bar{y}_{\cdot i})=\sigma_{u}^{-1}\left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{ti}-\delta\bar{y}_{\cdot i}\\
\vdots\\
y_{Ti}-\delta\bar{y}_{\cdot i}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{\cdot i}=T^{-1}e^{\prime}y_{i}=T^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}y_{ti}$
\end_inset

 is the mean of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th cross section unit.
 Each regressor in 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th unit will be transformed in the similar way.
\end_layout

\end_body
